From b9336ca651e10e4d4701459c32452e9324e2ad50 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Lamb <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 22:01:31 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] Servers: negotiate Electrum protocol 1.8

Offer protocol 1.8 from the draft "Electrum protocol: variable-length
block headers" beside 1.4 to 1.6, on every chain. A 1.8 client reads each
header's length from bit 31 of its version field (80 or 164 bytes) and
knows a v2 header's block is identified by its BLAKE2b hash. This is the
version Electrs Pruned speaks on the BLAKE2b chain, so a tool that works
with it gets the same here.

- server.version: a single version is accepted if it is 1.4 to 1.6 (as
  before) or exactly 1.8. A range gets the highest supported version in
  it: 1.8 if it reaches 1.8, otherwise the lower of its top and 1.6, and
  is refused if that is below its bottom. There is no 1.7, whose changes
  are not implemented: [1.4, 1.7] gets 1.6, and [1.7, 1.7] is refused.
  The decision is ServerMisc::NegotiateProtocol, a pure function with
  unit tests (tests/Protocol_tests.cpp).
- server.features: protocol_max is "1.8". protocol_min/protocol_max
  cannot say that 1.7 is missing; a client sending a range still gets
  the right answer.
- blockchain.block.headers: a list from 1.6 as before, but the
  concatenated hex string again at 1.8 (ServerMisc::HeadersAsList). A 1.8
  client must read both; Electrs Pruned at 1.8 sends the string, so a tool
  written against it may parse only that. The string branch already walks
  variable-length headers.

Clients below 1.8 are still served on a chain with v2 headers, as before;
this only adds a version.

ServerMisc::MaxProtocolVersion stays 1.6: PeerMgr judges other servers by
it and makeFeaturesDictForConnection is shared with PeerMgr, so peering
is not taught about 1.8 (Fulcrum Pruned runs with peering and announce
off). Fulcrum --version shows both.
